    body_en:
      "Host: New cities around the world seem to suffer from strikingly similar problems. Why does this keep happening?\nArchitect Jeong Min-seok: It's because urban planning relies too heavily on quantitative metrics like vehicle throughput. The assumption that wide roads and ample parking make an efficient city is already a half-century-old convention, yet it still survives as a design standard. As a result, the experience of pedestrians and cyclists gets pushed aside as a secondary concern.\nHost: What principles do successful alternatives follow, then?\nJeong: The projects I've worked on share one thing: they used human walking pace as the baseline for design from the very start. When shops, housing, and public facilities are arranged within a fifteen-minute walk, car dependency naturally drops. But for this principle to work, local politics must prioritize long-term quality of life over short-term development profits.\nHost: Is it possible to retrofit existing cities in this direction?\nJeong: It's certainly much harder than building a new city. Changing entrenched interests and existing road networks takes time and political courage. But it isn't impossible. I've seen several cities start with a small experiment — turning one street into a plaza — and gradually build trust from there.",
