    body_en:
      "Host: There often seems to be a gap between what climate models predict and the extreme weather people actually experience.\nDr. Han Ji-yeon: That's an accurate observation. Models are designed to capture average trends over decades, while people experience climate through individual events — this summer's heat wave, this winter's cold snap. The trouble is that scientifically, it's risky to flatly attribute any single event to climate change, and equally risky to flatly deny any connection.\nHost: What's a more effective way to communicate this to the public, then?\nDr. Han: I was trained to speak the language of probability, but I've realized that language doesn't map well onto everyday speech. So these days, instead of saying 'this event happened because of climate change,' I try to say 'climate change made an event like this several times more likely.' It's not perfect, but it's a compromise that conveys both causation and probability at once.\nHost: Doesn't admitting the models' limitations undermine public trust?\nDr. Han: I actually think the opposite. If uncertainty is hidden and later comes to light, trust collapses far more severely. I believe that honestly stating what models predict well and what they don't actually strengthens trust in science over the long run.",
