    body_en:
      "Host: Your company is often described using the phrase 'disruptive innovation,' yet I hear you're wary of that term yourself.\nFounder Choi Young-bin: That's right. The phrase sounds appealing to investors, but it doesn't accurately describe the actual work on the ground. What we did wasn't tear down an existing industry — it was closer to finding inefficiencies no one had bothered to notice and improving them bit by bit. The word 'disruption' erases that tedious, repetitive process.\nHost: Why do you think that kind of language became so widespread?\nChoi: Because the narrative of disruption is advantageous for raising investment. A story of revolutionary transformation attracts far more money than one of gradual improvement. But founders intoxicated by that narrative end up pouring their energy into crafting a dramatic story instead of solving real customer pain points.\nHost: What advice would you give founders pursuing sustainable growth, then?\nChoi: I'd tell them not to look for something to disrupt, but to look for a small inconvenience someone deals with every day. Our own business, in the end, started from reducing minor errors in shipping paperwork processing. It isn't glamorous, but I believe it's the only way to actually create value.",
